.TH PRINTENV 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
printenv \- print environment variables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B printenv
.RI [ name ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B printenv
writes environment variables to standard output.
With no
.IR name ,
it writes every variable, one per line, as
.IB name = value\fR.
The order is that of the process's environment: the order Windows
supplied the variables, followed by any that busybox added.
.PP
With one or more
.IR name s,
it writes the value of each variable in turn, followed by a LF, without
the name.
A name that is not set writes nothing and makes the exit status 1, and
the remaining names are still looked up.
A name may be repeated, and its value is written each time.
An empty name is never found.
A name that contains
.B =
is matched against a variable's name and value together: with
.B FOO
set to
.BR a=b ,
.B printenv FOO=a
writes
.BR b .
.PP
Values are written as they are, without quoting, so a value that
contains newlines spans several lines.
A variable with an empty value writes an empty line and counts as
found, so the exit status tells an empty variable from one that is not
set.
.PP
Names are not case-sensitive:
.B printenv path
writes the value of
.BR PATH .
.SH OPTIONS
.B printenv
parses no options.
Apart from a first argument of
.BR \-\-help ,
every argument, including
.B \-\-
and
.BR \-\-version ,
is a name.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The variables named as operands are read.
So are the following, which affect the lookup or the output.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime expands wildcards in the arguments when busybox starts,
quoted arguments too.
So when it is unset, as when
.B printenv
is run from
.B cmd.exe
or another program outside busybox,
.B printenv P*
looks up the names of the matching files and not the pattern.
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
When the variable is unset, busybox then sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so the listing includes
.BR BB_GLOBBING=0 .
.TP
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ", " BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
When standard output is a console, decide whether the console or busybox
interprets the escape sequences in the values, such as for color.
See
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B CONEMUPID
When it is set and neither of the above is, busybox uses terminal mode
0: it turns virtual terminal output off and emulates escape sequences
itself.
.TP
.B HOME
When it is unset,
.B printenv HOME
writes the user's profile directory, with forward slashes, though the
listing does not include it.
The name must be in capitals:
.B printenv home
writes nothing.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
When it is unset,
.B printenv TMPDIR
writes the value of
.BR TMP ,
or else of
.BR TEMP ,
though the listing does not include it.
As with
.BR HOME ,
the name must be in capitals.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every named variable was found, or no names were given.
.TP
.B 1
A named variable is not set, with no message.
Also standard output could not be written, with a message such as
.B "printenv: standard output: Bad file descriptor"
on standard error.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before the output was written,
and the output is too long for the C library's buffer.
No message is written.
Shorter output fails when
.B printenv
exits, with status 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write the value of one variable:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printenv PATH
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test whether a variable is set, even if its value is empty:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printenv EDITOR >/dev/null && echo set
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write several values, one per line, in the order asked.
The missing variable writes nothing and sets the status:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ FOO=1 BAR=2 printenv BAR FOO MISSING
2
1
$ echo $?
1
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the busybox-w32 variables that are set:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printenv | grep \(aq^BB_\(aq
BB_GLOBBING=0
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the environment with a NUL after each variable.
.B printenv
cannot do this, but
.B env
can:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ env \-0
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR printenv :
there are no options, so no
.B \-0
or
.BR \-\-null ,
and there is no
.BR \-\-version .
.B \-\-help
works only as the first argument.
.PP
Lines end with a bare LF, not CR LF.
.PP
Variables whose names begin with
.BR = ,
such as the
.B =C:
and
.B =ExitCode
entries that
.B cmd.exe
keeps, are not visible to
.BR printenv ,
neither in the listing nor by name.
.PP
Run from the kit's shell,
.B printenv
runs inside the shell process instead of starting a new one.
It sees the variables the shell has exported, and assignments on its own
command line, as in
.BR "FOO=1 printenv FOO" ,
but not the shell's unexported variables.
The listing is in the shell's own order.
The shell changes some of what it imports from Windows, such as the
case of names and the separators in values; see
.BR sh (1).
.PP
busybox makes its ANSI code page UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, so names and values may contain any Unicode character, and are
written to a file or pipe as UTF-8.
On older systems they are limited to the ANSI code page.
Output to a console is converted from the ANSI code page to the
console's code page.
Escape sequences in values, such as those for color, take effect, and
busybox emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR env (1),
.BR sh (1)
